(7599198, 1226854, 1031, '<td valign="top"><table align="center" width="95%"><tr><td><div class="textsm"><b>Originally posted by posthumous:</b></div><hr/><i> whatever you did to filter this is very interesting and compelling.<br/> <br/>I''m hanging this in my fantasy gallery<br/> <br/><a href="https://www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1077279" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/50000-54999/50695/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1077279.jpg"/></a> </i></td></tr></table> <br/>Thanks Don,<br/> <br/>It''s actually simple -- I set a custom White Balance using a Magenta swatch, which the camera uses to compensate by shifting everything toward the Green, which is "opposite" from Magenta on the "Color Wheel."<br/> <br/>I carry a 4x6 print with the primary Additive (RGB -- middle row) and Subtractive (CMY -- top row) colors just for this purpose -- feel free to download and use the print (third row of colors are arbitrary).<br/> <br/><a href="https://www.dpchallenge.com/image.php?IMAGE_ID=1153431" rel="nofollow" target="_blank"><img alt="'' . substr(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/0-4999/1031/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1153431.jpg'', strrpos(''//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/0-4999/1031/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1153431.jpg'', ''/'') + 1) . ''" border="0" src="//images.dpchallenge.com/images_portfolio/0-4999/1031/120/Copyrighted_Image_Reuse_Prohibited_1153431.jpg"/></a></td>', 'Originally posted by posthumous: whatever you did to filter this is very interesting and compelling. I''m hanging this in my fantasy gallery   Thanks Don, It''s actually simple -- I set a custom White Balance using a Magenta swatch, which the camera uses to compensate by shifting everything toward the Green, which is "opposite" from Magenta on the "Color Wheel." I carry a 4x6 print with the primary Additive (RGB -- middle row) and Subtractive (CMY -- top row) colors just for this purpose -- feel free to download and use the print (third row of colors are arbitrary). ', '2018-10-22 13:44:35.000000', NULL, 0),
